      "marks": 5,
      "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{1}{2(2t+1)(2t-1)(t+1)}$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "State $\\frac{dx}{dt}=2t-\\frac2{2t+1}$. B1. Use the correct quotient rule or product rule. M1; may first split $y$ as $\\frac12-\\frac1{4t+2}$. Obtain $\\frac{dy}{dt}=\\frac{(2t+1)-2t}{(2t+1)^2}$ OE, simplifying to $\\frac1{(2t+1)^2}$. A1. Use $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{dy}{dt}\\div\\frac{dx}{dt}$. M1; allow for $\\frac{dx}{dy}$. Obtain $\\frac1{2(2t+1)(2t-1)(t+1)}$. A1 OE; must have 1 in the numerator; may have $\\frac1{(2t+1)(4t^2+2t-2)}$; ISW. Alternative method: obtain $x=\\left(\\frac{y}{1-2y}\\right)^2-\\ln\\left(\\frac{2y}{1-2y}+1\\right)$. B1; OE, e.g. $x=\\left(\\frac{y}{1-2y}\\right)^2-\\ln\\left(\\frac1{1-2y}\\right)$. Use the correct quotient rule to differentiate. M1. Obtain $\\frac{dx}{dy}=\\frac{2y}{1-2y}\\left(\\frac{(1-2y)+2y}{(1-2y)^2}\\right)-\\frac2{1-2y}$. A1 OE. Express the reciprocal derivative in terms of $t$ to obtain $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac1{2(2t+1)(2t-1)(t+1)}$. M1 A1 OE. Total: [5]",
